      "math_academy_id": "q-158194",
      "sequence_position": 16,
      "problem": "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2})?$",
      "worked_solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n4-{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<4\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $<$ sign, the curve is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1$ and shade the region *inside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14246566/assets/59ac983361be7dd9a991e1cf134e7193256891a5587eab6bff5edf906fef3688.png)",
